1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,103
You open a terminal, and see a blinking cursor.

2
00:00:03,653 --> 00:00:06,447
No icons. No buttons. Only a prompt.

3
00:00:06,997 --> 00:00:10,261
Most servers have no screen at all, so this is how you work.

4
00:00:10,811 --> 00:00:13,280
Today you learn to move around: the shell,

5
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:14,456
the filesystem tree,

6
00:00:14,456 --> 00:00:14,808
paths,

7
00:00:14,808 --> 00:00:16,337
and a handful of commands.

8
00:00:18,387 --> 00:00:20,136
First, the terminal and the shell.

9
00:00:20,686 --> 00:00:24,249
The terminal is the window. The shell is the program inside it.

10
00:00:24,799 --> 00:00:26,356
The shell reads what you type.

11
00:00:26,906 --> 00:00:29,914
You type a command, press enter, and the shell runs it.

12
00:00:30,464 --> 00:00:33,238
On most Linux servers, the shell is called bash.

13
00:00:35,288 --> 00:00:36,632
Next, the filesystem.

14
00:00:37,182 --> 00:00:40,361
Linux organizes everything as one tree of folders.

15
00:00:40,911 --> 00:00:43,769
At the top is the root, written as a single slash.

16
00:00:44,319 --> 00:00:48,266
Inside it are folders such as home, etc, var, and tmp.

17
00:00:48,816 --> 00:00:51,589
Every file has exactly one place in this tree.

18
00:00:53,639 --> 00:00:54,578
Now, paths.

19
00:00:55,128 --> 00:00:57,432
A path is the address of a file in the tree.

20
00:00:57,982 --> 00:01:02,014
An absolute path starts at the root, with a slash. It works from anywhere.

21
00:01:02,564 --> 00:01:04,846
A relative path starts from where you are now.

22
00:01:05,396 --> 00:01:08,874
Two dots mean the parent folder. One dot means this folder.

23
00:01:09,424 --> 00:01:11,322
A tilde means your home folder.

24
00:01:13,372 --> 00:01:15,825
Three commands do most of the navigating.

25
00:01:16,375 --> 00:01:18,274
pwd prints where you are.

26
00:01:18,824 --> 00:01:20,275
ls lists what is here.

27
00:01:20,825 --> 00:01:22,446
cd takes you somewhere else.

28
00:01:22,996 --> 00:01:25,343
Where am I, what is here, and take me there.

29
00:01:27,393 --> 00:01:29,121
Four more commands change things.

30
00:01:29,671 --> 00:01:31,335
mkdir creates a folder.

31
00:01:31,885 --> 00:01:33,421
touch creates an empty file.

32
00:01:33,971 --> 00:01:36,766
cp copies, and mv moves or renames.

33
00:01:37,316 --> 00:01:40,174
rm removes, and there is no recycle bin.

34
00:01:40,724 --> 00:01:42,388
Check twice before you remove.

35
00:01:44,438 --> 00:01:46,870
Commands take options, written with a dash.

36
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:50,940
ls dash l gives a long listing, with sizes and dates.

37
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:55,501
ls dash a also shows hidden files, whose names start with a dot.

38
00:01:56,051 --> 00:01:58,717
When you forget, the man command shows the manual.

39
00:02:00,767 --> 00:02:04,394
Now let us see this for real, in a disposable Linux sandbox.

40
00:02:04,944 --> 00:02:08,144
These outputs are real captures, not an illustration.

41
00:02:08,694 --> 00:02:13,024
First: pwd shows where we are, and ls shows three items.

42
00:02:13,574 --> 00:02:18,460
Next, cd projects slash app goes down two levels, using a relative path.

43
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:24,471
Then cd dot dot, dot dot, slash logs goes up two levels and into logs.

44
00:02:25,021 --> 00:02:30,781
Last, a wrong path. The shell says: no such file or directory, and the exit status is two.

45
00:02:31,331 --> 00:02:33,934
A clear error message, and a number you can check.

46
00:02:35,984 --> 00:02:37,883
Three errors you will meet on day one.

47
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:43,211
No such file or directory: the path is wrong, or you are in a different folder.

48
00:02:43,761 --> 00:02:48,412
Permission denied: you are not allowed, so check who you are, and who owns the file.

49
00:02:48,962 --> 00:02:52,717
Command not found: a typo, or the program is not installed.

50
00:02:53,267 --> 00:02:56,595
First run pwd, then read the message slowly.

51
00:02:58,645 --> 00:03:00,501
Watch out for four common myths.

52
00:03:01,051 --> 00:03:03,987
Myth one: the terminal and the shell are the same.

53
00:03:03,987 --> 00:03:04,456
In fact,

54
00:03:04,456 --> 00:03:06,042
the terminal is the window,

55
00:03:06,042 --> 00:03:07,920
and the shell runs the commands.

56
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:13,868
Myth two: cd moves a file. In fact, cd changes your location, and nothing else.

57
00:03:14,418 --> 00:03:17,272
Myth three: a leading slash does not matter.

58
00:03:17,272 --> 00:03:17,791
In fact,

59
00:03:17,791 --> 00:03:20,775
it turns a relative path into an absolute one.

60
00:03:21,325 --> 00:03:25,826
Myth four: rm sends files to a recycle bin. In fact, they are gone.

61
00:03:26,376 --> 00:03:27,998
Read before you press enter.

62
00:03:30,048 --> 00:03:32,373
Here is how you check your own understanding.

63
00:03:32,923 --> 00:03:35,249
Imagine a teammate is lost in a terminal.

64
00:03:35,799 --> 00:03:37,527
Explain the terminal and the shell.

65
00:03:38,077 --> 00:03:39,826
Describe the tree, and the root.

66
00:03:40,376 --> 00:03:44,259
Explain absolute and relative paths, with one example each.

67
00:03:44,809 --> 00:03:48,307
Use pwd, ls, and cd to move around.

68
00:03:48,857 --> 00:03:50,457
Create a folder and a file.

69
00:03:51,007 --> 00:03:53,077
Read a no such file error, and fix it.

70
00:03:53,627 --> 00:03:55,248
To pass, you need all six.

71
00:03:57,298 --> 00:03:58,173
Your turn.

72
00:03:58,723 --> 00:04:02,349
Open a terminal on Linux or Mac, or use a Linux sandbox.

73
00:04:02,899 --> 00:04:06,462
One: run pwd, then ls, and note where you are.

74
00:04:07,012 --> 00:04:10,788
Two: make a folder called practice, go inside, and create a file.

75
00:04:11,338 --> 00:04:16,522
Three: return to your home folder using a relative path, then using an absolute path.

76
00:04:17,072 --> 00:04:18,672
Mark yourself on the rubric.

77
00:04:19,222 --> 00:04:20,950
Pause the video, and do it now.

78
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:35,375
Quick check.

79
00:04:35,925 --> 00:04:38,378
What is the difference between the terminal and the shell?

80
00:04:38,928 --> 00:04:42,192
What is the difference between an absolute and a relative path?

81
00:04:42,742 --> 00:04:45,217
Which three commands do most of the navigating?

82
00:04:45,767 --> 00:04:48,092
What does a no such file error usually mean?

83
00:04:48,642 --> 00:04:53,101
Objective covered: MC02-O03.

84
00:04:53,651 --> 00:04:56,424
